--TEST--
An attribute declared in a class is used only on that class's descendants and their members, so a use on an unrelated class, on the owner itself or on the owner's own method does not compile — `rule:attributes/an-attribute-is-declared-with-its-sites`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/an-attribute-is-declared-with-the-places-it-may-be-used
namespace Shop;

// The owner may not use its own attribute, on itself or on its own methods.
#[Storable::Index(columns: "id")]
abstract class Storable {
    attribute Index on class, method { columns: string }

    #[Storable::Index(columns: "id")]
    public function load(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

// A subclass and its method may use `Index`, so this class compiles.
#[Index(columns: "email")]
class Customer extends Storable {
    #[Index(columns: "name")]
    public function find(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

// `Mailer` does not extend `Storable`, so the qualified name does not help.
#[Storable::Index(columns: "id")]
class Mailer {}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E1006]: `Index` is declared in `Shop\Storable`, so it is used only on classes that extend or implement `Shop\Storable` and their members
  --> case.nvs:6:3
